      "writtenOn": "2023-01-16T15:37:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Isn\u0027t this just:\n\n    StructNlMsgHdr hdr \u003d StructNlMsgHdr.parse(bytes);\n    InetDiagMessage.parse(hdr, bytes);\n\n? Then you don\u0027t need the bytes.position() call. The length will be available as hdr.nlmsg_len and the type as hdr.nlmsg_type.\n\n\nIn fact, why write this code at all? Can\u0027t you just use the classes other code uses? I think it would be something like:\n\n    NetlinkMessage nlMsg \u003d (InetDiagMessage) NetlinkMessage.parse(buffer, NETLINK_INET_DIAG);\n    if (!(nlMsg instanceof InetDiagMessage)) {\n        // Log error...\n        return false;\n    }\n    InetDiagMessage inetDiagMsg \u003d (InetDiagMessage) msg;\n\n\nThat by itself wouldn\u0027t get you the attributes, which you need. But I think you could do that by adding them to InetDiagMessage, maybe something like this:\n\n     public class InetDiagMessage extends NetlinkMessage {\n         public StructInetDiagMsg inetDiagMsg;\n    +    public ByteBuffer nlAttrs;\n    \n\n         /**\n          * Parse an inet_diag_req_v2 message from buffer.\n          */\n         @Nullable\n         public static InetDiagMessage parse(@NonNull StructNlMsgHdr header,\n                 @NonNull ByteBuffer byteBuffer) {\n             final InetDiagMessage msg \u003d new InetDiagMessage(header);\n             msg.inetDiagMsg \u003d StructInetDiagMsg.parse(byteBuffer);\n             if (msg.inetDiagMsg \u003d\u003d null) {\n                 return null;\n    +        nlAttrs \u003d byteBuffer.slice()\n         }\n         return msg;\n     }\n\n\nThen you can parse the attributes (e.g., the mark) from inside msg.nlAttrs without worrying about changing the position of the netlink message. When NetlinkMessage#parse() returns, the bytebuffer will be at the correct position to read the new netlink message, if any.",
      "range": {
